WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.480
Welcome back to ScienceClic,&nbsp;
today, light cones and black holes.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.040
Imagine lighting a candle. The&nbsp;
candle emits a flash of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.640
that travels in all directions,&nbsp;
forming a bubble that grows.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:32.480
This bubble is in 3 dimensions, and surrounds&nbsp;
us completely. If we are inside this bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.800
it is impossible to get out of it because&nbsp;
we would have to exceed the speed of light.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.320
Imagine that we only perceive a 2D slice of space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.800
The bubble forms a circle&nbsp;
that grows all around us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:58.160
If we decompose the situation image by image,&nbsp;
and if we stack the images one after the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.640
we can construct a diagram in which the bubble of&nbsp;
light, which grows as time passes, forms a cone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.960
This cone widens from the past to the future, and&nbsp;
inside of it, we trace a trajectory through time.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.640
The sides of the cone are tilted at 45°,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.040
because for any given amount of time, light&nbsp;
always travels a set distance. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.080
in one year, light will always travel a&nbsp;
distance of “one light year” through space.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.840
This type of object is called a light cone. As&nbsp;
time passes, the cone expands at the speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.800
light, and whatever we do, our path remains&nbsp;
bounded by it, it’s impossible to escape it.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.160
In relativity, these light cones are essential&nbsp;
to understand the structure of the universe.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.720
In particular, they restrict&nbsp;
cause and effect relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:09.040
To understand, imagine an event that occurs&nbsp;
far from Earth, such as a supernova explosion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:15.840
This event won’t be able to affect us&nbsp;
until its light cone has reached Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.600
Before that, no information&nbsp;
about the explosion can reach us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.240
since nothing can be&nbsp;
transmitted faster than light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.600
This explosion can only be seen and felt on&nbsp;
Earth as soon as we enter its light cone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:41.360
“Causality,” the fact that one event can trigger&nbsp;
another, is limited by the speed of light.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.240
More importantly, light cones allow us&nbsp;
to understand the profound difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.280
between the notions of “time”, and “space”...

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:03.920
What is the difference between time and space&nbsp;
? Through space we can move in any direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.000
turn around, or trace a path&nbsp;
that loops back on itself...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:15.040
But through time, all objects are&nbsp;
forced to move in the same direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:23.200
In time, it is impossible to turn back, we&nbsp;
always move from the past towards the future.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.600
Light cones allow us to&nbsp;
clearly see this difference:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:33.440
through space, we are free&nbsp;
to choose any direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.040
but through time, the successive light&nbsp;
cones force us to always move forwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.600
We cannot turn around because we are&nbsp;
bound to remain within these lightcones.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:59.280
This property allows us to define rather&nbsp;
rigorously what we called “time”: “Time”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.080
in relativity, is simply the direction&nbsp;
in which the light cones are oriented.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:10.800
It is this direction along which we cannot&nbsp;
turn back. This is the direction towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:16.720
which all allowed trajectories point,&nbsp;
and in which our future necessarily lies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:27.840
Space on the other hand is the set of all&nbsp;
other directions, perpendicular to time.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.800
In an area where the universe is empty, with no&nbsp;
gravity, its structure, space-time, is straight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:42.480
Time and space form a symmetric, rectilinear grid,&nbsp;
and the light cones are all aligned in the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:55.840
direction. We can thus define "time" globally:&nbsp;
in this diagram, time goes from left to right.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.440
But imagine now that the universe contains a&nbsp;
very massive body, for instance the Earth. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:09.840
we drop an apple, the apple will gradually fall&nbsp;
downwards, deflected by the gravity of the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:18.720
However, gravity affects not only matter, but&nbsp;
also light. If the apple emits bubbles of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:26.800
over time, they will also be drawn&nbsp;
downwards. Close to a massive body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:34.080
the light cones are no longer aligned with each&nbsp;
other: they curve more and more, bent by gravity.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.200
Near a massive body, the direction&nbsp;
of the light cones curves...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.360
and in other words, "time" is bent&nbsp;
towards the center of the planet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:57.040
If the apple falls, although it was motionless at&nbsp;
first, it is because its future points downwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.080
Mass distorts the geometry of the&nbsp;
universe, space and time become relative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:19.840
their orientation depending on where&nbsp;
we are, this is “general relativity”.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.560
The more massive a body,&nbsp;
the more it bends spacetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:35.840
But imagine a massive object that is very compact.&nbsp;
Such a body would generate a curvature so strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:41.280
that below a certain altitude, all light&nbsp;
cones would be completely oriented downwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:48.480
If we were to light a candle in this place,&nbsp;
all light rays, whatever their direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:54.800
would be destined to fall towards the&nbsp;
center… this is what we call a black hole.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:59.680
A black hole is a spherical&nbsp;
region of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:06.000
a sort of bubble, in which the curvature of&nbsp;
space-time drives all objects towards the center.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:15.840
If we consider a 2D slice of space, the black&nbsp;
hole manifests as a circle, which stays static,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.360
and traces a cylinder through spacetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:24.640
The boundary of the black&nbsp;
hole is called the “horizon”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:34.080
Above the horizon some light rays can escape,&nbsp;
but below the horizon all light is captured.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:40.480
In a black hole, lightcones force&nbsp;
the path of any object downwards…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.160
"Time" itself points towards&nbsp;
the center of the black hole.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:55.520
To understand what a black hole really is, it is&nbsp;
wise to consider two different points of view:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:07:59.520
that of an astronaut who&nbsp;
falls into the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:03.760
and that of a distant observer,&nbsp;
stationed at a great distance.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:11.840
For the distant observer, gravity is very weak,&nbsp;
and in his vicinity the grid of spacetime is flat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:20.560
For him, time flows from left to right. The&nbsp;
horizon of the black hole seems motionless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:25.840
as it traces a straight line&nbsp;
from the past to the future.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.240
But let's now take the perspective&nbsp;
of the falling astronaut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.560
As she gets closer to the horizon,&nbsp;
“time” and “space” curve more and more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:43.200
bent by the presence of the black hole. When she&nbsp;
finally reaches the horizon of the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.880
the astronaut does not realize it, but, at&nbsp;
this point, time and space are tilted at 45°.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:57.680
For the astronaut, the horizon is not a&nbsp;
horizontal line from the past to the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:07.840
it’s a diagonal which rises at 45°…&nbsp;
exactly like the surface of a light cone.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:17.120
While the distant observer sees the black hole&nbsp;
as stationary, for the astronaut at the horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:22.320
time and space are tilted, such that the&nbsp;
black hole behaves like a light cone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:26.320
which explains why it is impossible to escape.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.880
When she crosses the horizon, the notions of&nbsp;
time and space seem swapped around compared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:41.760
the outside: time is now pointing downwards&nbsp;
- which, before, was a direction through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.320
space - the horizon of the black hole is&nbsp;
no longer a place in ​​space, but a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:56.000
in our past, and the center of the black hole is&nbsp;
no longer a point, but an event in our future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:03.520
a destiny we cannot avoid. Below the&nbsp;
horizon, all objects inevitably fall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:06.880
because it is in this direction&nbsp;
that their future lies.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.520
When a massive star collapses in on&nbsp;
itself, it emits one last flash of light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:26.240
a last bubble that tries to grow, but within a&nbsp;
curved spacetime, bent by the mass of the star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.760
such that the bubble seems&nbsp;
static from the outside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:39.440
A black hole has formed. It is a light cone...&nbsp;
rendered motionless by the curvature of spacetime.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:46.800
If we straighten back the&nbsp;
diagram, we recover a global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.120
direction of time, for flowing from left to right.

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:56.000
In this straightened diagram, we see explicitly&nbsp;
that the horizon of the black hole forms a light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.920
cone, emerging from the collapsing star, and from&nbsp;
the inside of which it is impossible to escape.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:07.680
Once below the horizon, we are forced to hit&nbsp;
the center of the black hole - a place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:15.840
the curvature becomes so intense that our models&nbsp;
no longer work. The center of the black hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.920
is an event… in the future.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:28.320
Finally, if we compactify this diagram, we&nbsp;
get a “Penrose diagram”, in which the outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:34.560
and the inside of the black hole form two&nbsp;
distinct regions. As soon as we cross the horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:41.120
the rest of the universe is behind us, in our&nbsp;
past. We won’t ever be able to access it again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:55.840
Our only possible future is to fall…&nbsp;
all the way to the singularity.

